  <h2>B. B3LYP molecular-level correction (DFT+Σ) — inputs</h2>
  <p>The semi-local functional (vdW-DF2) underestimates the molecular gap, so the transmission
     resonance is mis-placed at $E_F$. The correction is a <strong>DFT+Σ level alignment</strong>:
     each molecular orbital is shifted by $\Delta_i = \varepsilon_i^{\text{B3LYP}} -
     \varepsilon_i^{\text{vdW-DF2}}$ and added to the device Hamiltonian as</p>
  <p style="text-align:center">$\displaystyle \Delta H = S\,C\,\mathrm{diag}(\Delta_i)\,C^{\dagger}\,S,$</p>
  <p>with $S$ the overlap and $C$ the <strong>isolated-molecule</strong> MO coefficients — the
     in-device molecular block has no clean molecular levels here (Hf₂Se₉ is covalently fused into the
     chain), so an MPSH projector cannot be used; construction detail in
     <a href="../../methods/dft-sigma/index.html">Methods: DFT+Σ</a>. From the vacuum molecule
     (vdW-DF2 in SIESTA vs B3LYP in VASP, vacuum-level aligned) the gap opens
     <strong>0.02 → 0.75 eV</strong> with a strongly <strong>asymmetric</strong> shift — occupied
     levels drop ≈ 1 eV ($\Delta_\text{HOMO} = -1.01$ eV) but virtual levels only ≈ 0.1–0.3 eV
     ($\Delta_\text{LUMO} = -0.29$ eV). The device transport uses the same vdW-DF2, so the correction
     functional is consistent.</p>
