  <h3>Physics validity — honest read (is the number real?)</h3>
  <p>The corrected picture is <strong>qualitatively physical</strong> (occ↓/virt↑; molecular levels
     move off $E_F$ → $T$ suppressed) and the implementation is verified (level shift injected to
     87–111%). But the <strong>quantitative values are an upper bound / qualitative only</strong>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>gap 0.75 eV is a replay of the input</strong> — it <em>is</em> the isolated-molecule
      B3LYP gap that we injected, not a device prediction.</li>
    <li><strong>image-charge omitted → gap over-estimated.</strong> A molecule on a metal has its gap
      narrowed by the electrode image charge (Neaton PRL 97 (2006): benzene 10.5 → 7.2 eV; Fallaque
      JPCM 34 (2022): image charge "reduces the gap"). VSe₃ is metallic, so the real device frontier
      splitting is <em>smaller</em> than 0.75 eV and the true $T(E_F)$ suppression is <em>weaker</em>
      than 0.0032.</li>
    <li><strong>B3LYP-as-truth + non-self-consistent</strong> (molecular charge off by −0.6 e, no SCF
      feedback). Quantitative alignment needs image-charge-inclusive DFT+Σ or self-consistent GW.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="page-meta">Full derivation and reference quotes:
    <code>11-hetero60-transport/N25_9uc/0bias/dft_sigma_v2/docs/PHYSICS_VALIDITY.md</code>.</p>

