  <h3>Corrected Landauer I–V (zero-bias $T(E)$)</h3>
  <p>Landauer integral of the corrected $T(E)$ with a <strong>symmetric</strong> bias window
     (bias-independent $T(E)$ assumed):</p>
  $$I(V) \;=\; \frac{2e}{h}\int T(E)\,\big[f(E-\mu_L)-f(E-\mu_R)\big]\,dE,
  \qquad \mu_L = +\tfrac{eV}{2},\;\; \mu_R = -\tfrac{eV}{2},\;\; T = 300\ \mathrm{K}.$$
  <p>The correction suppresses the low-bias current (at $\pm0.1$ V: $1.93\to0.49$ μA;
     at $\pm0.5$ V: $5.94\to4.12$ μA). With a symmetric window and a bias-independent $T(E)$ the
     curve is odd, $I(-V) = -I(V)$ — a symmetric junction (VSe₃ on both sides) shows
     <strong>no rectification</strong> at this level; genuine rectification would require the true
     bias dependence $T(E,V)$ of a finite-bias calculation.</p>
