  <h2>Discussion / next</h2>
  <p><strong>What is actually implemented already:</strong> TBtrans already has the runtime hook for
    an additive Hamiltonian/self-energy correction, and sisl already has the file writer for that
    hook. The official TBtrans manual states that the electronic structure can be changed as
    $H \leftarrow H + \delta H + \delta\Sigma$, and defines <code>TBT.dH</code> as the fdf flag for
    the NetCDF4 file containing the $\delta H$ data. In sisl, <code>sisl.io.tbtrans.delta</code>
    implements the matching <code>.dH.nc</code> writer/reader: <code>write_delta</code> stores
    level-1 static terms when no <code>E</code> or <code>k</code> is supplied, and also supports
    k-dependent, E-dependent, and E+k-dependent delta files.</p>
  <p><strong>What is not automatic:</strong> there is no project-specific DFT+Σ button that knows our
    molecular orbitals or B3LYP shifts. We still have to construct the actual molecular projector
    correction, $\Delta H = S\,C\,\mathrm{diag}(\Delta_i)\,C^\dagger S$, write that matrix with
    sisl as a level-1 <code>deltaH.dH.nc</code>, then tell TBtrans to use it with
    <code>TBT.dH deltaH.dH.nc</code>. The source-patch part is unnecessary because the file-based
    δH insertion path already exists; the physics/mapping step is still our implementation task.</p>
